+## Finite readiness wait (P03 prerequisite)
+
+`wait.rs` provides one finite `poll` operation over at most 16 borrowed
+native-layout descriptors, with a validated timeout of 0 through 1000 ms.
+Fractional milliseconds round upward; this quantization and kernel scheduling
+are not a render-deadline guarantee. Interruptions return to the owner without
+retry, so its original deadline cannot be silently restarted. Ready counts and
+returned flags are checked together; hangup, error and invalid-descriptor flags
+remain faults for the owner to handle, not successful I/O. Oversized arrays are
+refused without scanning them; output is meaningful only on success.
+
+`PlatformLayer::wait_once` consumes that policy only when its admitted matrix
+specifically qualifies poll. Admission of epoll alone cannot authorize it.
+The Linux leaf uses the existing ABI tables through `wait_route.rs` and issues
+one raw syscall, without changing socket admission or adding an alternate
+reactor. The new wrapper adds one audited unsafe call inside the existing
+`sys` exception; no application crate gains unsafe code.
+
+This connects the platform layer to the readiness primitive P03 needs. It does
+not implement the Asupersync reactor/timer bridge, nor qualify production
+playback. The older hello guest's poll probe does not establish execution of
+this new wrapper. Guest/stock-kernel execution and runtime integration remain
+separate qualification requirements; scripted host tests cannot supply them.
